    Task 50/447: P8 — typed DSM atomic / SpinLock / CondVar wrapper module
    
    Build the first cut of the P8 contract module
    `src/am/common/dsm.rs` with typed wrappers around PostgreSQL DSM
    atomics, SpinLocks, and condition variables. The wrappers carry an
    explicit lifetime tied to the DSM segment the caller asserts is
    live; construction is the only unsafe surface, and the methods on
    each wrapper are safe and encapsulate the underlying PG primitive.
    
    Module surface:
    
      - PgAtomicU32Ref<'a>: load_acquire / store_release /
        compare_exchange_acqrel_acquire over pg_atomic_uint32
      - SpinLockGuard<'a>: RAII guard with Drop-on-release
      - spinlock_init / condition_variable_init: one-shot in-place init
        helpers for fields embedded in DSM-laid-out structs
      - ConditionVariableRef<'a>: signal() over PG condition variables
    
    First migration: PgLockedDsmInsertStateCell in HNSW parallel build
    collapses its three internal unsafe blocks (read_u32 / write /
    compare_exchange) into a single shared `as_atomic_ref()` borrow,
    saving two unsafe blocks. The SpinLockGuard / ConditionVariableRef
    wrappers are landed but not yet consumed — preserving wide
    compound spinlock blocks as a single unsafe block is currently a
    narrower-block-count choice than splitting them; future slices
    will revisit when surrounding context allows compound migration.
    
    Per-file delta:
      build_parallel.rs   114 -> 112  (-2)
      (new) common/dsm.rs  0 -> 9 unsafe blocks (in encapsulated primitives;
                                     not counted against HNSW subsystem)
    
    Cumulative HNSW rotation delta: -222 (549 -> 327, -40.44%).
    
    Note: the dsm.rs module's internal `unsafe { ... }` blocks sit
    under `src/am/common/`, not `src/am/ec_hnsw/`, so they do not
    count against the HNSW subsystem total. This is the intended
    disposition per the execution plan §P8: "DSM pointer arithmetic
    and atomic field access removed from HNSW/common call sites."
    
